ntfs-macos patch 0004: test-only fault-injection device layer (`./configure --enable-faultio`)

Rationale (T-M3-14, S-CRASH-*, U-VMK-07, TEST_STRATEGY.md sections 6 and 7): the crash-consistency
sweep and the volmark crash table need to stop the device after write N, tear write N, fail it with
EIO, or slow it down, on a real ntfs-3g mount. NTFS-3G has no such hook, so this patch adds one
under the device layer.
Hunks:
  - include/ntfs-3g/faultio.h (new): everything lives here. Without ENABLE_FAULTIO it defines three
    macros (FAULTIO_WRITE, FAULTIO_PWRITE, FAULTIO_FSYNC) that expand to exactly the upstream
    write(), pwrite() and ntfs_fsync() calls. With ENABLE_FAULTIO it defines static functions in
    unix_io.c and an environment-driven plan (the plan grammar, the NTFSMAC_FAULT_STATE counter
    file and the optional NTFSMAC_FAULT_KILL=1 self-kill at the first dropped write are described at
    the top of the header): count, cut:N, eio:N, dead:N, short:N:K, skip:N, delay:MS. NTFSMAC_FAULT_TRACE logs
    offset, length and magic of every write. STATE and TRACE are opened with O_NOFOLLOW and every
    NTFSMAC_FAULT_* variable is ignored in a setuid or setgid process; plan numbers that overflow
    are rejected; the kept bytes of a torn write are written in a loop.
    NTFSMAC_FAULT_PLAN unset means no behaviour change even in a faultio build.
  - libntfs-3g/unix_io.c: five single-line edits, no line added or removed: one blank line becomes
    the #include, and the write(), pwrite() and the two ntfs_fsync() call sites use the macros.
    Because no line moves, the release object file of unix_io.c is byte-identical in .text and
    .rodata to the one built without this patch (checked by tests/stress/crashsweep/build.sh, which
    also runs nm over the release libntfs-3g.a, ntfs-3g and ntfsfix).
  - configure.ac, configure, config.h.in: the option --enable-faultio (default no) defines
    ENABLE_FAULTIO. The tarball ships a generated configure, so configure and config.h.in are
    edited by hand with the text autoconf produces for the neighbouring --enable-debug option.
    Regenerating configure with autoreconf gives the same result from configure.ac.
Scope: device layer only; nothing touches allocation, runlists, MFT or index code. The layer sits
below ntfs_pwrite(), so every library user (ntfs-3g, ntfsfix, mkntfs, the probe) is affected the same
way and only in a faultio build.
Safety: release builds must never pass --enable-faultio. The layer drops or fails writes on purpose;
the build script's default configure line does not contain the option, and build.sh in
tests/stress/crashsweep proves with nm that the release artefacts carry no trace of it.
Upstream status: not submitted (test infrastructure specific to ntfs-macos).
Test: S-CRASH-* (tests/stress/crashsweep), U-VMK-07 (T-M3-12).

